# Python style guide

## Project structure

- Use Poetry and `pyproject.toml`; add console scripts for CLIs under
  `[tool.poetry.scripts]`.
- Source layout: `src/<package>/`; never implicit namespace packages.
- Pin a minimum Python version in `pyproject.toml` (`python = "^3.12"`).
- Separate runtime deps from dev deps (`[tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies]`).
- Lock file (`poetry.lock`) is committed; CI runs `poetry install --frozen`.

## Type annotations

- All public functions carry complete type annotations.
- `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every file
  (enables forward references without import cycles).
- Run `mypy --strict` on production modules; disable only with a
  per-line comment and a WHY comment explaining the exception.
- Prefer `X | None` over `Optional[X]`; prefer `X | Y` over `Union[X, Y]`.

## Imports

- No star imports (`from module import *`).
- One import per line inside each import block.
- Order: stdlib → third-party → local. `ruff` enforces this.
- Never use `importlib.import_module` or `__import__` for runtime
  dynamic dispatch — fix the design instead (see
  `languages/clojure-no-requiring-resolve` for the same principle in Clojure).

## Naming

- `snake_case` for modules, functions, variables, parameters.
- `PascalCase` for classes and type aliases.
- `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for module-level constants (see
  `foundations/named-constants` — the same intent-bearing rule applies).
- Private helpers: single leading underscore (`_helper`); no double
  underscores except for genuine dunder methods.
- No abbreviations unless universally understood (`url`, `id`, `db`).

## Functions and pure design

- Write pure functions wherever possible: same inputs → same outputs,
  no implicit state reads.
- Keep each function to one level of abstraction (Stratified Design —
  `foundations/stratified-design`).
- No mutable default arguments:

  ```python
  # BAD
  def append(item, lst=[]):
      lst.append(item)
      return lst

  # GOOD
  def append(item: object, lst: list[object] | None = None) -> list[object]:
      result = lst if lst is not None else []
      result.append(item)
      return result
  ```

- Prefer data classes over plain dicts for structured data:

  ```python
  from dataclasses import dataclass

  @dataclass(frozen=True)
  class JobRecord:
      job_id: str
      url: str
      status: str
  ```

  `frozen=True` enforces immutability; drop it only when mutation is
  genuinely required and document why.

## Error handling

- Raise specific exceptions, not bare `Exception` or `BaseException`.
- Never silence exceptions with a bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass`. At minimum log the error and re-raise, or return an explicit failure value.
- Chain exceptions with `raise NewError("...") from original` so the
  traceback is preserved.
- At I/O boundaries, validate inputs before doing work and raise
  `ValueError` / `TypeError` with a human-readable message (see
  `foundations/validation-boundaries`).
- For recoverable failures, prefer returning a value that encodes
  success/failure over exceptions (aligns with
  `foundations/result-handling`):

  ```python
  # Preferred at internal boundaries
  def parse_config(raw: str) -> tuple[Config, None] | tuple[None, str]:
      ...
  ```

- Do not use `try`/`except` for flow control.

## Testing

- Framework: `pytest -q`; no `unittest.TestCase` unless wrapping legacy code.
- Test files mirror the source tree: `src/foo/bar.py` → `tests/foo/test_bar.py`.
- One `assert` per logical assertion; use `pytest.raises` for expected exceptions.
- Use `@pytest.fixture` for shared setup; keep fixtures small and focused.
- No `#` commented-out test functions — delete them or open an issue.
- Parameterize repeated cases with `@pytest.mark.parametrize` rather than
  hand-rolled loops.
- Never `@pytest.mark.skip` without a tracking issue reference in the reason string.
- Test module-level constants and pure functions directly; mock only at
  explicit I/O boundaries.

## Anti-patterns

| Avoid | Use instead |
|-------|-------------|
| `except Exception: pass` | explicit failure value or re-raise |
| Mutable default arguments | `None` sentinel |
| `print()` for logging | `logging` or `structlog` |
| `os.system()` / `subprocess.call()` with shell=True | `subprocess.run([...])` with `shell=False` |
| Global mutable state | pass state explicitly or use a context object |
| `type()` equality check | `isinstance()` |
| Magic string literals throughout code | module-level constants (Dewey 006) |
| `raise NewError(...)` without chaining | `raise NewError(...) from original` |

## Linting and formatting

- `ruff` for linting and formatting (replaces flake8 + black + isort).
- `mypy --strict` for type checking.
- Add both to CI; pre-commit hooks enforce both locally.
- `pyproject.toml` is the single config source for all tools:

  ```toml
  [tool.ruff]
  line-length = 100
  select = ["E", "F", "I", "UP", "B", "SIM"]

  [tool.mypy]
  strict = true
  ```
